“Learning to Visualize: Machine Learning-Driven Adaptation and Perceptual Evaluation of Non-Planar Public Data Displays”

As interactive public displays become more embedded in our environments, their effectiveness depends not just on the technology, but also on how people perceive and interact with them — especially when moving beyond traditional flat screens to non-planar, ambient, and spatially embedded surfaces. To advance this, I propose to integrate machine learning into both the design and evaluation phases of the project.

This would allow us to:

→ Learn from user interaction and perceptual feedback to optimize visualization layouts on curved or flexible surfaces.

→ Create adaptive systems that dynamically choose what content to display based on context and user engagement.

→ Use behavioral data to train models that classify or predict user needs or preferences in public settings.

→ Explore how ML can augment tangible visualizations, making them responsive and intelligent over time.
